---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CapabilityGrid from '../components/CapabilityGrid.astro';
import ProblemGrid from '../components/ProblemGrid.astro';
import InvestigationWorkflow from '../components/InvestigationWorkflow.astro';
import CTA from '../components/CTA.astro';
import { site } from '../config/site';
import { pg14Facts } from '../data/facts';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="PostgreSQL Performance Consultant — DatabaseDeveloper"
  description={`Fixed-scope PostgreSQL performance investigation for SaaS teams on RDS, Aurora or self-managed. Read-only evidence, ranked findings, report in ${site.investigationTurnaround}.`}
>
  <div class="container hero">
    <div class="hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">PostgreSQL performance for SaaS teams</span>
        <h1>Production PostgreSQL performance, investigated from evidence—not guesses.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          For SaaS engineering teams dealing with rising latency, high database cost, query regressions,
          locking, connection pressure or risky PostgreSQL upgrades.
        </p>
        <p class="notice hero-deadline">
          <strong>PostgreSQL 14 stops getting fixes on {pg14Facts.communityEol}.</strong>
          On RDS and Aurora, Extended Support charges start {pg14Facts.rdsExtendedSupportYear1Start}.
          <a href="/postgresql-14-eol">See the dates →</a>
        </p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button primary" data-track="primary_cta_click" href="/contact">Request an investigation</a>
          <a class="button ghost" data-track="hero_upgrade_click" href="/postgresql-upgrade-readiness">PG14 upgrade readiness</a>
        </div>
        <p class="small">Done by me, Kiran Sabne: about 8 years running production PostgreSQL, Aurora/RDS, SQL Server and Snowflake. <a href="/about">More about me →</a><br /><a data-track="sample_report_click" href="/sample-report">See a sample report →</a></p>
        {site.foundingOffer.enabled && (
          <p class="small muted">Founding investigation: {site.foundingOffer.international} for the first three suitable engagements, covering {site.foundingOffer.scope}.</p>
        )}
        <p class="small muted">One cluster and its dominant workload · client-run, read-only evidence · written findings and review call</p>
      </div>
      <aside class="hero-proof">
        <span class="eyebrow">The deliverable</span>
        <h3>A decision your team can use</h3>
        <ul class="signal-list">
          <li>Ranked findings linked to workload evidence</li>
          <li>Trade-offs across reads, writes and concurrency</li>
          <li>Implementation risk and rollback considerations</li>
          <li>Before-and-after checks for meaningful changes</li>
        </ul>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Symptoms</span><h2>Start with what production is doing.</h2></div>
        <p>Performance is rarely one isolated SQL statement. The useful question is which evidence explains the workload behavior and what change is safe to test first.</p>
      </div>
      <ProblemGrid />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Method</span><h2>Evidence → judgment → validation.</h2></div>
        <p>AI can explain an execution plan. The investigation is about what surrounds that plan: workload concentration, locks, connections, statistics, indexing and deployment context.</p>
      </div>
      <InvestigationWorkflow />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Deliverable</span><h2>A ranked change plan, not a dump of findings.</h2></div>
        <p>Every finding is tied to evidence, confidence, risk and a validation step so your engineering team can make an informed change.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid three">
        <div class="card"><span class="kicker">Evidence</span><h3>Why this finding exists</h3><p>Plans, workload statistics, waits/locks, schema/indexes and relevant configuration.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="kicker">Decision</span><h3>What matters first</h3><p>Findings are ranked by expected impact, confidence, risk and effort—not by how impressive they sound.</p></div>
        <div class="card"><span class="kicker">Validation</span><h3>How to know it worked</h3><p>Each recommendation includes a measurable verification path and rollback consideration.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">Capabilities</span><h2>PostgreSQL first. Broader where the workload demands it.</h2></div>
        <p>PostgreSQL is the core service. Specialist reviews cover transactional, vector and analytical workloads where the problem calls for them.</p>
      </div>
      <CapabilityGrid />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head">
        <div><span class="eyebrow">See the work</span><h2>Review the method before you get in touch.</h2></div>
        <p>Explore two reproduced PostgreSQL problems and a sample report. The captured plans, waits and timings show how evidence leads to a safer decision.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid two">
        <a class="card card-link" href="/case-studies/lab-generic-plan-regression">
          <span class="kicker">Measured lab</span><h3>Generic plan regression under prepared statements</h3>
          <p>A role-level plan setting slowed small tenants. See the plan evidence and tested response.</p>
        </a>
        <a class="card card-link" href="/case-studies/lab-lock-queue">
          <span class="kicker">Measured lab</span><h3>DDL lock queue behind an idle transaction</h3>
          <p>A catalog-only ALTER TABLE stalled eight readers for 14 seconds. See the blocking chain and timeout test.</p>
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted">Both examples are synthetic labs using generated data; their measurements are not client results.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="container"><CTA /></div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
